path: content/dotnet/csharp/lambda-unsubscribe-does-nothing.yaml
stub: {"stubId": "csharp-13", "topic": "csharp", "slug": "lambda-unsubscribe-does-nothing", "level": "middle", "type": "find-bug", "subtopic": "lambdas and events: unsubscribing", "angle": "In a snippet that subscribes a lambda to an event, later writes `-=` with an identical lambda and raises the event, find the line that fails to unsubscribe the handler.", "answerHinge": "Each lambda expression creates a new delegate instance, so `-=` with a textually identical lambda finds no match and the original handler keeps running; the delegate must be stored in a variable to be removed.", "sources": ["https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/events/how-to-subscribe-to-and-unsubscribe-from-events"]}
Generator notes: Claim that event `-=` on a field-like event ultimately uses Delegate.Remove (via Interlocked.CompareExchange loop in the generated remove accessor) and that Remove returns the source unchanged when no equal delegate is found; verify against the Delegate.Remove docs page.; Claim that two textually identical lambdas compile to two distinct methods (and that even compiler caching of non-capturing lambdas is per lambda site), so the delegates are never equal. Here both lambdas capture `calls` and share the same closure object as target, but the methods differ.; Expected outputs (buggy prints 2, fixed prints 1) were traced by hand, not executed; run-snippets should confirm. bugLineSets [[5]] assumes line 5 is the `-=` line (recounted: 1 Publisher, 2 int calls, 3 +=, 4 Raise, 5 -=).; Assumed the Microsoft Learn how-to page explicitly recommends storing an anonymous function in a variable to unsubscribe later; the link to Delegate.Remove (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.delegate.remove) is expected to exist but should be opened to confirm.
Mechanical evidence:
- executed in the sandbox; declared {"kind": "output", "value": "1"}; actual {"kind": "output", "value": "1"}
- warning: link unknown: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/events/how-to-subscribe-to-and-unsubscribe-from-events (429)
Numbered code.source (quote every line listed in bugLineSets):
  1 | Publisher publisher = new();
  2 | int calls = 0;
  3 | publisher.Changed += (sender, e) => calls++;
  4 | publisher.Raise();
  5 | publisher.Changed -= (sender, e) => calls++;
  6 | publisher.Raise();
  7 | Console.WriteLine(calls);
  8 | 
  9 | class Publisher
 10 | {
 11 |     public event EventHandler Changed;
 12 | 
 13 |     public void Raise() => Changed?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
 14 | }
